      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "We have recently shown that gabapentin generates protein kinase A (PKA)-dependent presynaptic inhibition of GABAergic synaptic transmission in locus coeruleus (LC) neurons only under neuropathic states. To verify behaviorally this in vitro electrophysiological finding, the PKA inhibitor H-89 was injected intracerebroventricularly (i.c.v.) before supraspinal application of gabapentin in mice developing thermal and mechanical hypersensitivity after peripheral nerve injury. H-89 dose-dependently attenuated the analgesic effects of i.c.v.-injected gabapentin, suggesting that PKA-dependent removal of GABAergic inhibition of LC neurons is the most plausible synaptic mechanism underlying the supraspinally mediated analgesic effects of gabapentin involving activation of the descending noradrenergic pain-inhibitory system."},
